  "text": "Design a pipeline for X. Expect it in a data engineer interview, next to sequel that goes a level deeper than an analyst screen. Interviewers probe four things, from the query down to the files. Advanced sequel: window functions, qualify, deduplication, gaps and islands, and incremental logic. Data modeling: star or snowflake schemas, facts and dimensions, normalization trade-offs, and slowly changing dimensions. Pipelines and orchestration: batch or streaming, idempotency, backfills, and scheduling in Airflow or Dagster. And systems and formats: columnar files like Parquet, partitioning and file sizes, warehouse or lakehouse, and cost.\n\nAI has changed this interview in four ways. Ingestion for retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG, is now a system design prompt: parse, chunk, embed, index, and keep it fresh incrementally, like any other pipeline. Vector search trade-offs: exact or approximate, Postgres or a dedicated store, metadata filters, and hybrid keyword search. Governance for AI data: permissions checked at query time, deletions that reach the vectors, and personal data kept from outside embedding APIs. And AI-written pipeline code needs review for idempotency, time zones and schema drift. Some coding rounds now allow an assistant, so ask the recruiter about each one.",
